1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,335
Four journeys. No traveller here is a real
person: each is a composite,

2
00:00:05,403 --> 00:00:07,194
built on published facts.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,188
Riyadh, late evening.

4
00:00:10,256 --> 00:00:14,806
A family drives its car onto a train and
finds its sleeper cabin.

5
00:00:14,874 --> 00:00:17,749
They wake in the far north, at Qurayyat.

6
00:00:18,099 --> 00:00:22,134
That part is real: North Line night trains
have sleeper cabins,

7
00:00:22,201 --> 00:00:25,065
and carry cars between Riyadh and
Qurayyat.

8
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:29,197
Thursday afternoon. A consultant in Riyadh

9
00:00:29,265 --> 00:00:32,365
heads for the airport, as most weeks, for
Jeddah.

10
00:00:32,715 --> 00:00:38,819
In 2025, Jeddah–Riyadh was fifth in OAG's
top ten air routes by scheduled seats:

11
00:00:38,887 --> 00:00:43,953
about 9.8 million both ways. Seats are
capacity, not passengers.

12
00:00:45,367 --> 00:00:50,615
Jeddah. A family packs for a summer week
in Abha, up on the escarpment.

13
00:00:50,965 --> 00:00:56,428
By road, passes like Aqabat Shaar: 11
tunnels, 32 bridges. By air,

14
00:00:56,496 --> 00:01:02,864
one airline's published schedule listed
Abha to Jeddah at 1 hour 15 minutes.

15
00:01:02,932 --> 00:01:04,340
By train, no way.

16
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:07,012
In January 2025,

17
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:13,168
the head of Asir's development authority
gave a formal target of 9.1 million

18
00:01:13,236 --> 00:01:15,160
visitors a year by 2030.

19
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:22,405
Back in Riyadh, a student finishes her
last exam and books a seat home to Tabuk.

20
00:01:22,473 --> 00:01:27,016
Fly, or drive the long road north-west: no
train goes there.

21
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:33,767
Four journeys, one question: what would
make rail a real choice on more of them?

22
00:01:33,835 --> 00:01:38,199
An independent study tried to answer, in
ranges, not promises.

23
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:43,064
The Hejaz Railway reached Madinah in 1908;

24
00:01:43,132 --> 00:01:48,331
the line in what is now Saudi Arabia
remained closed after 1920.

25
00:01:49,733 --> 00:01:50,857
In October 1951,

26
00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:55,352
King Abdulaziz inaugurated the
Riyadh–Dammam railway line.

27
00:01:56,767 --> 00:02:02,068
Today's passenger lines: the East Line to
Dammam, and the North Line,

28
00:02:02,136 --> 00:02:06,234
about 1,250 kilometres via Qassim, Hail
and Al-Jouf,

29
00:02:06,301 --> 00:02:10,467
with passenger trains reaching Qurayyat
since 2022.

30
00:02:11,867 --> 00:02:17,807
The Haramain line links Makkah, Jeddah and
Madinah: open since October 2018,

31
00:02:17,875 --> 00:02:21,480
at up to 300 kilometres an hour from early
2020.

32
00:02:22,867 --> 00:02:27,054
And Riyadh has a metro: six lines and 85
stations,

33
00:02:27,122 --> 00:02:31,273
opened between December 2024 and January
2025.

34
00:02:32,667 --> 00:02:35,290
Who is off this map? The study

35
00:02:35,357 --> 00:02:40,110
screened all 150 governorates. In the 2022
census,

36
00:02:40,177 --> 00:02:47,060
Taif had about 913 thousand people; Tabuk,
624 thousand; Khamis Mushait,

37
00:02:47,127 --> 00:02:53,785
601 thousand; Abha and Najran, 422
thousand each; Jazan, 201 thousand.

38
00:02:53,853 --> 00:02:58,673
None has a passenger station within 10
kilometres.

39
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:04,660
Committed lines, the Landbridge and the
Saudi section of the GCC Railway,

40
00:03:04,728 --> 00:03:08,937
are announced or under procurement. None
carries passengers yet.

41
00:03:10,333 --> 00:03:11,558
Even with them,

42
00:03:11,626 --> 00:03:17,425
about 26.10 of 35.93 million people live
within two hours of a station:

43
00:03:17,493 --> 00:03:20,178
a reported count, never scored.

44
00:03:21,567 --> 00:03:24,985
The country is growing. The

45
00:03:25,053 --> 00:03:30,205
2022 census counted 32,175,224 people.

46
00:03:30,555 --> 00:03:36,515
By mid-2024, the General Authority for
Statistics estimated 35.3 million.

47
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:40,223
After The Line launch in July 2022,

48
00:03:40,291 --> 00:03:45,912
His Royal Highness Crown Prince Mohammed
bin Salman told journalists the aim is 50

49
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:50,859
to 60 million people in the Kingdom by
2030, about half Saudis. Remarks,

50
00:03:50,927 --> 00:03:53,318
not a published programme target.

51
00:03:54,733 --> 00:03:57,958
For Riyadh, the Royal Commission's
president

52
00:03:58,026 --> 00:04:00,998
told Reuters in July 2020 the idea was a
city

53
00:04:01,066 --> 00:04:05,879
of 15 million by 2030. Later reports say
that figure is under review.

54
00:04:07,266 --> 00:04:10,102
The Vision 2030 document itself sets this:

55
00:04:10,170 --> 00:04:13,006
'To increase our capacity to welcome Umrah

56
00:04:13,074 --> 00:04:16,462
visitors from 8 million to 30 million
every year.'

57
00:04:16,811 --> 00:04:18,988
And: 'To have three Saudi cities be

58
00:04:19,056 --> 00:04:22,616
recognized in the top-ranked 100 cities in
the world.'

59
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,054
It speaks of the need 'to complete,

60
00:04:26,121 --> 00:04:30,370
improve and link our infrastructure
internally and across borders',

61
00:04:30,437 --> 00:04:35,192
and of 'constructing shared road and
railway networks' with the Gulf states.

62
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,129
A growing country raises a fair question:

63
00:04:39,197 --> 00:04:42,684
should airports and highways be the only
ways across it?

64
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:47,314
Who would ride? Business on the
Riyadh–Jeddah trunk;

65
00:04:47,381 --> 00:04:51,902
families heading for the cool of Asir;
students and families going north.

66
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:58,392
The busiest pair first. In 2050, with the
Landbridge running,

67
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:05,040
rail carries about 0.42 million of 4.66
million one-way trips a year between the

68
00:05:05,108 --> 00:05:09,275
Riyadh and Jeddah metros; air about 3.47
million.

69
00:05:10,666 --> 00:05:13,410
Why so few? In the corridor sketches,

70
00:05:13,478 --> 00:05:17,672
air stays ahead door to door in every rail
case modelled,

71
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:23,930
even non-stop at 350 kilometres an hour.
Rail's case rests on the towns in between,

72
00:05:23,998 --> 00:05:26,084
and city-centre stations.

73
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,571
This film's thread: connecting the
country,

74
00:05:30,639 --> 00:05:34,288
not only its holy cities and largest
metros. The test:

75
00:05:34,356 --> 00:05:39,064
who can reach one of five big metros
within 120 minutes, door to door?

76
00:05:40,466 --> 00:05:45,687
None of the layouts tested brings any new
place within 60 minutes of a big metro.

77
00:05:45,754 --> 00:05:48,639
The distances are national, not suburban.

78
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:52,650
Does a railway create trips, or move them?

79
00:05:52,717 --> 00:05:56,791
The committed lines move trips between
modes and destinations;

80
00:05:56,859 --> 00:06:00,157
the national total is the same with or
without them.

81
00:06:01,567 --> 00:06:05,060
If cheaper, faster travel also creates
trips,

82
00:06:05,128 --> 00:06:09,078
the model adds 0.2 to 1.1 million trips a
year in 2050,

83
00:06:09,145 --> 00:06:12,341
which barely changes the overall result.

84
00:06:13,733 --> 00:06:17,425
Today, air carries much of the country: in
2025,

85
00:06:17,493 --> 00:06:23,065
the civil aviation authority counted 65
million domestic travellers,

86
00:06:23,132 --> 00:06:25,482
of about 140.9 million in all.

87
00:06:26,867 --> 00:06:33,676
OAG named Jeddah–Riyadh the
fastest-growing route in its 2025 top ten:

88
00:06:33,744 --> 00:06:37,641
up 13 percent on 2024, 22 percent on 2019.

89
00:06:39,033 --> 00:06:43,653
A caution from Japan: despite an extensive
high-speed rail network,

90
00:06:43,721 --> 00:06:46,401
the world's second and third busiest air

91
00:06:46,469 --> 00:06:49,621
routes in 2025 were Japanese domestic
routes.

92
00:06:51,033 --> 00:06:57,310
In December 2025, Saudi Arabia and Qatar
agreed a 785-kilometre high-speed line,

93
00:06:57,377 --> 00:07:00,712
with stops including Hofuf and Dammam, and
a

94
00:07:00,779 --> 00:07:04,733
target trip of about 2 hours. Announced,
not built.

95
00:07:06,133 --> 00:07:11,497
The Landbridge, about 900 kilometres of
new railway between Riyadh and Jeddah,

96
00:07:11,565 --> 00:07:15,700
is reported as due by 2034. It too is
announced, not existing.

97
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:21,631
How do you test a railway that does not
exist? Start with the ground:

98
00:07:21,699 --> 00:07:27,298
each hypothetical line was routed over
real terrain, on 30-metre elevation data,

99
00:07:27,365 --> 00:07:30,568
at least cost through slopes, dunes and
wadis.

100
00:07:31,967 --> 00:07:36,345
On three lines that already run, lengths
came within 5 percent,

101
00:07:36,412 --> 00:07:39,616
but paths sat 3 to 10 kilometres off on
average.

102
00:07:39,684 --> 00:07:42,787
So every route here is a sketch, not a
design.

103
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:50,281
Tunnels, viaducts and earthworks were
measured. Off the Taif escarpment,

104
00:07:50,349 --> 00:07:56,431
two of the via-Taif sketches turn 61.9
straight-line kilometres into 91.4

105
00:07:56,498 --> 00:08:01,476
kilometres of track, with 16.2 of tunnel
and 14.8 of viaduct.

106
00:08:02,867 --> 00:08:05,586
Near Makkah, one rule shaped the sketches:

107
00:08:05,653 --> 00:08:09,843
a through Riyadh–Jeddah train stays
outside the Haram boundary,

108
00:08:09,910 --> 00:08:13,403
so every ticketed passenger can complete
the journey;

109
00:08:13,471 --> 00:08:16,799
Haramain already serves Makkah, from Ar
Rusayfah.

110
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:23,734
Costs come from the study's own register,
for double-track electrified railway,

111
00:08:23,801 --> 00:08:28,510
not from press-release totals. Each is a
range: low, middle and high.

112
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:32,390
Big rail projects often overrun,

113
00:08:32,458 --> 00:08:37,324
so costs carry an uplift from
international reference classes,

114
00:08:37,391 --> 00:08:43,878
one of 355 rail projects, with contingency
reported separately, not quietly added.

115
00:08:45,267 --> 00:08:48,289
Then the search: on top of the committed
lines,

116
00:08:48,357 --> 00:08:52,487
combinations of 71 candidate corridors:
new stations, spurs,

117
00:08:52,555 --> 00:08:54,694
commuter belts and trunk links.

118
00:08:56,100 --> 00:09:00,653
Each candidate was scored by one frozen
appraisal model, the judge,

119
00:09:00,721 --> 00:09:06,004
over 10,000 random draws, its fingerprint
checked before and after every run.

120
00:09:06,072 --> 00:09:10,125
The score is cautious: the worst of three
population cases.

121
00:09:11,533 --> 00:09:16,324
Classical optimisers searched, and so did
an automated research loop:

122
00:09:16,392 --> 00:09:21,332
one change at a time, a written
hypothesis, kept only if the judge agreed.

123
00:09:22,733 --> 00:09:29,281
Every attempt left a row in a register:
47,108 rows in the final search.

124
00:09:30,667 --> 00:09:35,278
At every checkpoint, a person read the
results and decided what came next.

125
00:09:35,346 --> 00:09:37,912
The machine searched; it did not decide.

126
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:44,433
Does it pay back? For every riyal spent,
how many come back? Below one,

127
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:48,557
a line does not repay its cost. For the
committed lines,

128
00:09:48,625 --> 00:09:50,956
the middle answer is about 0.3.

129
00:09:52,367 --> 00:09:58,543
Three options were put forward; none is a
recommendation to build. The central case:

130
00:09:58,611 --> 00:10:04,523
new stations at Al Jubayl, Dhahran and
Duruma, costing 0.8 to 5.3 billion riyals.

131
00:10:04,591 --> 00:10:10,571
In the middle case, each riyal brings back
about 6; even at high cost, it pays back.

132
00:10:11,967 --> 00:10:17,818
The upside case adds a spur to Al-Kharj,
costing 6.2 to 41.3 billion riyals;

133
00:10:17,886 --> 00:10:23,925
in the middle case, each riyal brings back
about 2.3. But if costs come in high,

134
00:10:23,992 --> 00:10:28,314
it no longer pays back in two of three
population cases.

135
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:33,626
The reach alternative adds a Riyadh
commuter belt,

136
00:10:33,694 --> 00:10:39,942
putting 4.25 million people within 120
minutes, door to door, of the five metros,

137
00:10:40,010 --> 00:10:45,515
against 3.56 million. Each riyal brings
back about 1.1, just above one;

138
00:10:45,582 --> 00:10:49,019
at high cost, it does not pay back in any
case.

139
00:10:50,433 --> 00:10:53,565
Who pays? No option pays for itself from
fares:

140
00:10:53,633 --> 00:10:59,145
tickets cover only part of running and
renewal costs, so however it is delivered,

141
00:10:59,213 --> 00:11:01,346
the state pays the difference.

142
00:11:02,733 --> 00:11:06,596
What comes back? For the committed lines
over 60 years,

143
00:11:06,664 --> 00:11:11,552
the model puts users' travel time and cost
savings, in today's money,

144
00:11:11,620 --> 00:11:15,226
at 2.3 to 19.3 billion riyals, and a
safety benefit,

145
00:11:15,294 --> 00:11:20,353
road deaths and injuries avoided valued at
standard appraisal values,

146
00:11:20,421 --> 00:11:21,959
at 0.6 to 7.4 billion.

147
00:11:23,366 --> 00:11:27,246
A much bigger figure, the wider boost to
the economy,

148
00:11:27,314 --> 00:11:32,898
is roughly 114.6 to 267.7 billion riyals
in the middle case across the three

149
00:11:32,966 --> 00:11:37,115
options. The method is unreliable on these
large zones,

150
00:11:37,182 --> 00:11:40,502
so the study keeps it outside every
ranking.

151
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:47,149
And a direct Riyadh–Jeddah railway? A side
study, not the official ranking,

152
00:11:47,217 --> 00:11:50,555
sketched five routes in place of the
Landbridge.

153
00:11:50,623 --> 00:11:56,121
Each returns only about 0.2 per riyal: on
these numbers, none pays back its cost.

154
00:11:56,189 --> 00:12:01,173
That helps explain why there is no direct
Riyadh–Jeddah railway today.

155
00:12:02,566 --> 00:12:04,036
They differ in reach.

156
00:12:04,104 --> 00:12:08,393
The direct sketch adds no one to the
national two-hour reach;

157
00:12:08,460 --> 00:12:11,126
the three via Taif about 0.23 million;

158
00:12:11,194 --> 00:12:16,405
the chain through the Landbridge's
announced stops about 0.31 million.

159
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:22,749
Within 120 minutes of the five metros,
only two add anyone:

160
00:12:22,817 --> 00:12:27,663
about 93,693 people with a gateway
south-west of Makkah,

161
00:12:27,731 --> 00:12:31,314
and about 109,007 with a stop at Al-Jumum.

162
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:38,312
Now the far north and south: Tabuk, Abha,
Khamis Mushait, Najran and Jazan.

163
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:43,428
On today's map, none has a passenger
station within 10 kilometres.

164
00:12:44,833 --> 00:12:50,581
As a comparison, not a recommendation, the
study also built one connected network:

165
00:12:50,649 --> 00:12:53,889
the cheapest 16 additions that join 18
cities.

166
00:12:55,300 --> 00:12:59,609
In it, a line of about 460 kilometres runs
from Tabuk to Sakaka,

167
00:12:59,677 --> 00:13:04,828
then a short spur to the North Line. Abha
and Khamis Mushait link to Najran,

168
00:13:04,895 --> 00:13:10,114
through Bisha to Taif and the Landbridge,
and down the mountains to Jazan.

169
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:16,052
That puts 32.7 million people within two
hours of a station,

170
00:13:16,119 --> 00:13:20,277
against 26.1 million with the committed
lines alone.

171
00:13:21,666 --> 00:13:26,118
Door to door by train, how long from these
cities to the nearest big city?

172
00:13:26,186 --> 00:13:28,446
The study did not route those trips.

173
00:13:29,833 --> 00:13:33,803
The price of one connected network: in the
middle case,

174
00:13:33,871 --> 00:13:37,821
each riyal brings back only about 0.06,
far below one.

175
00:13:39,233 --> 00:13:42,820
A field survey and observed demand could
change all of this;

176
00:13:42,888 --> 00:13:47,639
stage gates before each construction start
mark where to revisit the choice.

177
00:13:49,033 --> 00:13:52,741
Four journeys. A night train north that
already runs.

178
00:13:52,808 --> 00:13:57,774
An air shuttle that rail does not beat. A
climb to Abha and a road to Tabuk,

179
00:13:57,842 --> 00:13:59,436
still off the rail map.

180
00:14:00,833 --> 00:14:04,255
A railway that connects more of the
country is possible.

181
00:14:04,322 --> 00:14:09,303
Whether it is worth building is for those
who decide; the evidence is here to read,

182
00:14:09,371 --> 00:14:11,375
every figure sourced on screen.
